Yes — in Reactor/Spring WebFlux you should **wait for the `Flux<String>` to complete**, combine all emitted chunks, then save the final value.

`@HttpExchange` / `@PostExchange` can be used as a declarative HTTP client method, and returning `Flux<String>` is appropriate for a streamed response. Reactor’s `Flux.collectList()` collects all emitted items and emits a `Mono<List<T>>` **only when the Flux completes**. ([docs.enterprise.spring.io](https://docs.enterprise.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/integration/rest-clients.html?utm_source=openai))

## 1. If you only want to save after the full stream is received

```java
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class MathService {

    private final MathClient mathClient;
    private final MathResultRepository repository;

    public Mono<MathResult> callServiceAndSave(MathRequest request) {
        return mathClient.calculate(request)     // Flux<String>
                .collectList()                  // Mono<List<String>> after stream completes
                .map(chunks -> String.join("", chunks))
                .flatMap(fullData -> {
                    MathResult entity = new MathResult();
                    entity.setResult(fullData);
                    return repository.save(entity);
                });
    }
}
```

Repository example:

```java
public interface MathResultRepository
        extends ReactiveCrudRepository<MathResult, Long> {
}
```

Spring Data reactive repositories return reactive types such as `Mono`/`Flux`, so `repository.save(...)` should be chained with `flatMap`, not called separately. ([docs.spring.io](https://docs.spring.io/spring-data/relational/reference/r2dbc/repositories.html?utm_source=openai))

---

## 2. If your controller should still return the stream to the caller, but save after completion

If you want to **stream data back to the client immediately** and also save the full accumulated response when the stream finishes, do this:

```java
@GetMapping(value = "/math", produces = MediaType.TEXT_EVENT_STREAM_VALUE)
public Flux<String> math(MathRequest request) {
    return mathService.streamAndSave(request);
}
```

Service:

```java
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class MathService {

    private final MathClient mathClient;
    private final MathResultRepository repository;

    public Flux<String> streamAndSave(MathRequest request) {
        return Flux.defer(() -> {
            StringBuilder builder = new StringBuilder();

            return mathClient.calculate(request)
                    .doOnNext(chunk -> builder.append(chunk))
                    .concatWith(Mono.defer(() -> {
                        String fullData = builder.toString();

                        MathResult entity = new MathResult();
                        entity.setResult(fullData);

                        return repository.save(entity)
                                .then(Mono.empty());
                    }));
        });
    }
}
```

Important: use `Flux.defer(...)` so each request/subscription gets its own `StringBuilder`.

---

## 3. Do **not** use `subscribe()` inside your service

Avoid this:

```java
mathClient.calculate(request)
        .collectList()
        .subscribe(data -> repository.save(...)); // bad
```

In WebFlux, you should return the `Mono` or `Flux` and let Spring subscribe. Manually subscribing can cause hidden execution, lost errors, and lifecycle problems.

---

## 4. If you use blocking JPA instead of reactive R2DBC

If your database call is blocking, for example with `JpaRepository`, do not run it directly on the WebFlux event loop. Wrap it on `boundedElastic`:

```java
public Mono<MathResult> callServiceAndSave(MathRequest request) {
    return mathClient.calculate(request)
            .collectList()
            .map(chunks -> String.join("", chunks))
            .flatMap(fullData ->
                    Mono.fromCallable(() -> {
                        MathResult entity = new MathResult();
                        entity.setResult(fullData);
                        return jpaRepository.save(entity);
                    }).subscribeOn(Schedulers.boundedElastic())
            );
}
```

But if your whole app is reactive, prefer **R2DBC** or another reactive database driver.

---

### Short answer

Use:

```java
flux.collectList()
    .map(list -> String.join("", list))
    .flatMap(fullData -> repository.save(new MathResult(fullData)));
```

If you need to **return the stream and save at the end**, use `doOnNext` to accumulate and `concatWith` to save after completion.
